*The 2-adic finite transition.* The sum over primitive closures uses the same graph decomposition as in §C.6, now for finite quadratic modules over $ZZ_2$. For an added cyclic factor, enumerate its actual subgroups and every possible image of a fixed generator in the preceding quotient. Require the annihilator relation and cancellation of the quadratic values. Reducing the isotropic graph gives the next quotient; replacing a coordinate of least valuation gives an overlattice basis. Orbits of actual isometries may be used with their sizes because they preserve all these conditions and the residual form. Equal repeated factors have the corresponding multinomial multiplicity.

The elementary part requires a separate orbit justification. For $delta=0$, use Witt's theorem on the non-degenerate quadratic space over $FF_2$. For $delta=1$, diagonalize as
$ E=chevron.l 1 slash 2 chevron.r^a perp chevron.l 3 slash 2 chevron.r^b. $
To absorb a binary integer-valued plane with basis $u,v$, $b(u,v)=1 slash 2$, take an orthogonal vector $c$ of norm $1 slash 2$. The three vectors $c+u,c+v,c+u+v$ are an orthogonal basis. Their norms are $1 slash 2,1 slash 2,3 slash 2$ for the hyperbolic plane, and $3 slash 2,3 slash 2,3 slash 2$ for the other plane. The case $q(c)=3 slash 2$ reverses the signs. On four coordinates of the same sign, the matrix $I+J$ over $FF_2$ reverses all four signs, where every entry of $J$ is 1. Thus dimension and the Brown invariant $a-b$ modulo 8 determine the needed diagonal representative.

Put $B=2b$. In diagonal coordinates it is the standard dot product. The following are actual isometries: permutations of coordinates with equal coefficient; $x |-> x+B(x,w)w$ for $q(w)=1$; and, on two positive and two negative coordinates, $(I+J)P$, where $P$ interchanges the positive and negative pairs. For the last transformation, the norm numerators of the four coordinates sum to 8, and each column is the complement of a basis vector of the opposite sign. Its columns therefore have the original norms and are pairwise $B$-orthogonal.

Modulo equal-coefficient permutations, a vector is represented by its two support sizes $(i,j)$. A transvection with support $(r,s)$ and overlap $(u,v)$ acts, when $u+v$ is odd, by
$ (i,j) |-> (i+r-2u,j+s-2v), quad r+3s equiv 2 med (mod 4). $
All feasible overlaps are included. For the four-coordinate isometry the targets are $(i-u+v,j-v+u)$ for even overlap and $(i-u+2-v,j-v+2-u)$ for odd overlap. The finite graph on these support pairs is connected for each fixed norm after separating zero and the characteristic vector $(a,b)$, for every $1<=a+b<=20$ and every $a,b$ #calc("C18"). Each support pair represents $binom(a,i)binom(b,j)$ vectors. Consequently each such class is a single orbit, and those sizes justify the compression used by the finite transition. The zero-dimensional case is immediate. The bound 20 covers the present root inputs and their residual generator numbers; no assertion for arbitrary dimension is needed.

*The 3-adic outer sum.* With a single order-9 factor $A=chevron.l v chevron.r perp W$, $q(v)=2a slash 9$ with $3 divides.not a$, every isotropic subgroup is killed by 3: an element of order 9 has a unit norm numerator modulo 3, which the elementary part cannot cancel. Put $K=chevron.l 3v chevron.r$. If an isotropic subgroup contains $K$, it is $K$ plus an isotropic subspace $U$ of $W$, and its reduction removes the order-9 factor. If it avoids $K$, it is the graph of a map $U -> K$, with $3^(dim U)$ choices, and the reduction retains an order-9 factor together with the Witt reduction of $W$. With more than one order-9 factor the code enumerates actual finite subgroups and their orthogonal quotients. This outer sum over primitive closures is distinct from the primitive-gluing count below.

